          "reasoning": "I found no sign of an attack. This looks like a developer building and testing their own code on app-01. Order of events: the ubuntu user created and edited preload_fix.c by hand (touch, then nano, at 11:46). They renamed it to prog.c and compiled it with gcc/ld, which wrote /home/ubuntu/prog at 11:48:17. Two minutes later the same user ran 'sudo ./run_hook.sh'. That started 'sh ./dll_hook.sh', which ran ./prog as root. Root came from a normal sudo by the account that owns the home directory, not from an exploit. The graph shows no persistence, no writes to system paths and no suspicious outbound activity coming from the chain. Caveat: the script names (dll_hook.sh, run_hook.sh) and the 'preload' file name point to LD_PRELOAD hooking, which attackers also use. I did not read the source or script contents or check what the sudo process's network connections were, so this conclusion rests on the process chain alone.",
